## 3. Database Strategy — Polyglot Persistence

### 3.1 PostgreSQL — Relational Data (Source of Truth)
**Used for:** Users, Roles, Employers, Companies, Subscriptions, Audit logs, Analytics

```sql
-- Core schemas
users            (id, firebase_uid, email, role, is_active, created_at)
user_profiles    (user_id, display_name, phone, photo_url, location)
employers        (id, user_id, company_id, job_title, department)
companies        (id, name, slug, website, logo_url, verified, plan_tier)
subscriptions    (id, company_id, plan, start_date, end_date, is_active)
audit_logs       (id, user_id, action, resource, ip_address, timestamp)
job_approvals    (id, job_id, admin_id, action, note, created_at)
analytics_events (id, user_id, event_type, payload::jsonb, timestamp)
```

**Why PostgreSQL here:**
- ACID compliance for user/payment/audit data
- Joins for relational admin reports
- JSONB columns for flexible event payloads

### 3.2 MongoDB — Document Store
**Used for:** Jobs, Applications, Kanban boards, AI results, Chat history, Scraped jobs, Profiles

```javascript
// Collections
jobs             { _id, title, company, location, salary, tags[], requirements,
                   description, job_type, work_mode, posted_at, expires_at,
                   source, source_url, status, employer_id, applicants_count,
                   scraped_at, is_scraped }

applications     { _id, job_id, candidate_id, employer_id, stage, applied_at,
                   resume_url, cover_letter, ats_score, stage_history[],
                   employer_notes[], timeline[] }

kanban_boards    { _id, candidate_id, columns: { SAVED:[], APPLIED:[],
                   SCREENING:[], INTERVIEW:[], OFFER:[], REJECTED:[] } }

candidate_profiles { _id, user_id, headline, bio, skills[], experience[],
                     education[], certifications[], languages[], resume_urls[],
                     preferred_job_types[], preferred_locations[], salary_range,
                     profile_completion_pct, availability }

employer_profiles  { _id, company_id, about, industry, team_size, culture[],
                     benefits[], office_photos[], social_links{} }

ai_results       { _id, user_id, job_id, resume_url, ats_score: { overall,
                   skill_match, experience_fit, keyword_density, formatting,
                   keywords[], suggestions[] }, computed_at }

chat_sessions    { _id, user_id, messages[{ role, content, timestamp }],
                   session_type, created_at }

scraped_raw      { _id, source, url, raw_html, normalized{}, scraped_at,
                   processed, job_id }
```

**Why MongoDB here:**
- Flexible schemas for job descriptions (vary massively by source)
- Nested documents for timeline/stage history
- Full-text search via Atlas Search or Elasticsearch integration
- Fast horizontal scaling for high-read job listings

### 3.3 Redis — Cache + Queue + Sessions
**Used for:**
- **Session / Token cache**: Firebase token validation cache (5 min TTL)
- **Job listings cache**: Public job search results (2 min TTL)
- **Celery broker**: Task queue for AI jobs, scraping, emails
- **Redis Streams**: Real-time event streaming between services
- **Rate limiting**: Per-IP and per-user sliding window counters
- **Leaderboard**: Job popularity, skill demand index

